# Sparky Stereo OS's own packages win over Debian's (the stereo kernel, NVIDIA's open modules
# with our patches, KWin and KScreen with stereo 3D, the edition's packages), so an update
# never swaps the stereo stack for stock packages; we rebuild them when Debian moves.
# Installed by the sparky-stereo-apt package as /etc/apt/preferences.d/sparky-stereo (its one
# owner on an installed system); the development VM copies this same file there.
Package: *
Pin: release o=Sparky Stereo OS
Pin-Priority: 1001

# Bino comes only from Sparky Stereo OS (Daniel, 2026-10-06): our build decodes any input
# but always outputs full side by side, the edition's contract with KWin. Debian's and
# deb-multimedia's Bino have their own output modes (anaglyph, interleaved, half side by
# side...), which would hand KWin a picture outside the contract, so they never install, on any
# architecture: apt_preferences(5) matches a plain name on the native architecture only, ":any" on all
# of them (ORDER 318).
Package: bino:any
Pin: release o=Debian
Pin-Priority: -1

Package: bino:any
Pin: release o=Unofficial Multimedia Packages
Pin-Priority: -1

# deb-multimedia's keyring: the repository carries deb-multimedia's own package (its 2024.9.1, only repackaged through
# Lean Code as 2024.9.1+lc1), so that sparky-stereo-apt can depend on it before deb-multimedia is in the sources. Once
# it is, deb-multimedia's own package, which follows its key, is preferred: this one is only the way in.
# sparky-stereo-only-ours does not block deb-multimedia's (NOT_OURS in apt/make-only-ours.py, ORDER 321).
Package: deb-multimedia-keyring
Pin: release o=Sparky Stereo OS
Pin-Priority: 400
